// Regression test: a for-loop whose 'step' clause touches a variable OTHER
// than the induction variable must compile (this is legal, if suspicious,
// code -- 'for' doesn't require step to touch its own variable) and should
// emit a StuckForLoopVariableWarning rather than silently producing a real
// infinite loop with no diagnostic at all. See Lint.cpp's LintFor() and
// MayModifyVariable(), and Errors.h's StuckForLoopVariableWarning doc
// comment for the full story -- this exact shape (`step j += 10` never
// touching `i`) was the concrete example that motivated the check.
//
// This file intentionally does NOT execute the loop body (the point is to
// verify the compiler detects and warns about the stuck variable, not to
// actually hang the test suite) -- 'for' is deliberately never reached at
// runtime by returning before it.

fn main(): int {
    mut j: int = 0;
    if (true) {
        return 0;
    }
    for i in 0..10 step j += 10 {
    }
    return 0;
}
